---
import ProviderPlaybookTemplate from '../components/ProviderPlaybookTemplate.astro';
const data = {
 "kind": "troubleshooting",
 "name": "cPanel",
 "title": "cPanel DNS Problems: Common Issues and Fixes",
 "description": "The most common cPanel DNS problems, from broken mail to SERVFAIL, with the diagnosis and fix for each.",
 "intro": "Most cPanel DNS complaints collapse into the same handful of causes. Match your symptom below; every fix includes the free tool that confirms it.",
 "issues": [
  [
   "Emails fail after adding an addon domain",
   "The zone was created without mail records; re-add MX (and webmail CNAME) in Zone Editor for that domain."
  ],
  [
   "Zone Editor shows records that do nothing",
   "Nameservers point elsewhere (Cloudflare etc.): edit records there instead."
  ],
  [
   "Serial number errors after manual edits",
   "cPanel manages serials; hand-edited zone files are the usual culprit for stale secondaries."
  ]
 ],
 "quirk": "Hosting providers often run cPanel AND the registrar role; changing nameservers at the host-registrar to their own defaults is usually a one-click preset.",
 "faqs": [
  [
   "Is cPanel DNS separate from my registrar?",
   "Yes: cPanel is hosting software. If your domain is registered elsewhere and pointed at the host's nameservers, cPanel's Zone Editor manages it."
  ],
  [
   "How long do DNS changes take in cPanel?",
   "The provider serves new values quickly, but resolvers keep old answers until each record's TTL expires. Verify live values with the record lookup, then watch the propagation checker until resolvers agree."
  ],
  [
   "Where do I find cPanel's nameservers?",
   "This playbook's nameservers guide covers the exact location. They are the delegation pair your registrar must point at for cPanel to be authoritative."
  ],
  [
   "Should I lower TTL before changing records in cPanel?",
   "Yes, for anything planned: set TTL to 300 seconds at least one old-TTL cycle ahead, make the change, confirm propagation, then restore the TTL."
  ],
  [
   "Can I keep email with one provider and DNS in cPanel?",
   "Yes: DNS host and mail provider are independent. Publish the mail provider's MX, SPF, DKIM, and DMARC records in cPanel, and keep those records unproxied."
  ],
  [
   "Which records should every domain have?",
   "A (and ideally AAAA) for the website, MX plus SPF/DKIM/DMARC for mail, and CAA to restrict certificate issuance. Everything else is per-service."
  ]
 ],
 "related": [
  [
   "DNS record types reference",
   "/dns-records/"
  ],
  [
   "TTL explained",
   "/what-is-ttl-in-dns/"
  ]
 ]
};
---
<ProviderPlaybookTemplate data={data} />
